Yeah, so there's a difference in there being an intended referent for 
subject matter and an overt one for icons. I don't get how that affects 
whether imaginary-world quantification is sufficient or not to handle 
it. Sure, subject matter can include stuff you completely make up, with 
no referent in this world; but if you read enough fiction, so can 
icons. A tree branch can look like an elf too. In fact, it's the book 
rather than the branch that is likelier to have an individual referent: 
the book is about a specific elf, the branch looks like Any elf.



So maybe depiction is intensional in the Any-x sense after all.



